<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sviatoslav I of Kiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 942–<a href="/years/972">972 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Sviatoslav I, Sviatoslav · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sviatoslav I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> was a prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a> in the tenth century. In the 960s he campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, destroying their power. Accounts of the Khazar Khaganate treat his campaigns as decisive in the khaganate's decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Sviatoslav I of Kiev" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yevgeny Lansere · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sviatoslav_by_Eugene_Lanceray_1886.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sviatoslav was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igor-of-kiev">Igor of Kiev</a>, who was killed by the Drevlians about 945. His mother Olga ruled as regent until he reached maturity, around 963. Almost nothing is known of his youth, which he spent reigning in Novgorod, and he was tutored by a Varangian named Asmud. He had little patience for administration and lived with his druzhina in near-constant warfare. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> he carried no wagons or kettles on campaign and slept on a horse-blanket with his saddle under his head. His mother converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a> in 957, but he remained a pagan, and the Primary Chronicle says he feared his warriors would mock him if he converted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Shortly after he took power, Sviatoslav began campaigning in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> valley and the Pontic steppe. He rallied the East Slavic vassals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, forced the Vyatichs to pay tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> rather than the Khazars, and attacked Volga Bulgaria. He destroyed the Khazar city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarkel">Sarkel</a> around 965 and later the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atil">Atil</a>, though the order of events is disputed. He did not occupy the Khazar heartland, and Khazar successor statelets lingered, but the destruction of Khazar power opened the north-south steppe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> trade routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>.</p><p>In 967 or 968 the Byzantine emperor Nikephoros Phokas paid him 15,000 pounds of gold to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. He defeated the Bulgarian ruler Boris II and occupied northern Bulgaria, but he refused to hand the conquests to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and decided to move his seat to Pereyaslavets on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. In 969 he divided his lands among his sons Yaropolk, Oleg and Vladimir and invaded Bulgaria again, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a> and massacring its inhabitants.</p><p>The new emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John Tzimiskes</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardas-skleros">Bardas Skleros</a>, who defeated the coalition at the Battle of Arcadiopolis, and then marched on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> himself. Sviatoslav was besieged at Dorostolon for sixty-five days and agreed to leave the Balkans in return for food and safe passage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On the way home, in early 972, Sviatoslav was ambushed and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> rapids at Khortytsia, after, according to the chronicle, he ignored the warning of Sveneld. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> says the Pecheneg khan made his skull into a chalice. His conquests were not consolidated, and his sons fought one another: Oleg was killed in 976 and Yaropolk in 978, and Vladimir became sole ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. The campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, leaving it open to attack by Basil the Bulgar-Slayer four decades later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sviatoslav I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Byzantium encouraged Prince <b>Sviatoslav of Kiev</b> to attack Bulgaria in 968, and he took much of the country, including Preslav, and planned to settle on the Danube.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 960s Sviatoslav's campaign against the Khazars, waged by Prince <b>Sviatoslav I of Kiev</b> probably with Oghuz allies, ended in the capture of Sarkel around 965 and the sack of Atil, which in effect destroyed the khaganate's political structure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Oleg and his successors Igor, Olga and <b>Sviatoslav I</b> extended tribute over East Slavic tribes such as the Polianians, Drevlians, Severians and Krivichians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Primary Chronicle, the Pecheneg Khan Kurya made a chalice from the skull of <b>Sviatoslav I of Kiev</b>, in accordance with the custom of steppe nomads.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The decline of Khazaria after the campaign of <b>Sviatoslav I of Kiev</b> in 965 freed the Bulgars from tribute and allowed full independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Olga had ruled Kiev as regent for her son <b>Sviatoslav</b> after 945 and converted to Christianity; Yaroslav the Wise married his sister and three daughters to the kings of Poland, France, Hungary and Norway, and his granddaughter Eupraxia, the daughter of Vsevolod, married the emperor Henry IV.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kievan-rus">Social Hierarchy of Kievan Rus'</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in Dnieper Rapids, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.45,30.52&amp;year=961" aria-label="Kyiv on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5247 411 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sviatoslav I of Kiev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5697 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5697 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.45,30.52&amp;year=961">What was here in 961 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
